Goal:
The goal is to find which ports are open by usnig nmap and use the current level 
password to retrive the ssh private key for the next level.

Connect:
ssh bandit16@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

Approach:
First thing would be to use nmap on the port range that they have specified to 
understand what services are working on what port.

Using the -sV option we can see that some ports in that range are just echo 
services which just echo what we input.

with a alittle trial and error we can get the password on port 31790.

Command:

nmap -sV -p 31000-32000 localhost
openssl s_client --connect localhost:31790
(or)
ncat -ssl localhost 31790

Breakdown:

The first nmap command is used to identify what services are 
running on the the range of port specified.

There are two command that can be used to perform the 
same operation both connect to localhost on the port specified.

Result:

The password is revealed by entering the current password on the right port 
in localhost which is (127.0.0.1) in IPv4 notation.

Key Takeaway:

As I had basics of networking and nmap the port scanning part of this room was
the easiest part of the room for me.

The openssl part of the room was also easy as it was pretty standard from the
prev rooms.
